  "record_id": "015",
  "problem": "Channel additivity violations",
  "collection": "Original research investigation",
  "summary": "The largest integer strictly less than x is ceil(x)-1, not ceil(x).",
  "review_status": "source_reviewed",
  "review_note": "No open interpretation blocker was recorded in the preceding source review. This is not a new independent mathematical verification.",
  "original": {
    "excerpt": "Since \\(D_i\\le\\Delta_{\\rm flat}<HE\\), equations (1.6) and (2.4) give\n\n$$\nm_i<\n\\frac{H\\,127^2K^2}\n{126^2(K-H)S_i}.\n$$\n\nThe exact strict-integer ceilings are\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n(m_i)=(&60996,48631,44023,40693,39789,38265,39011,\\\\\n       &38265,39789,40693,44023,48631,60996).\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\tag{2.6}\n$$",
    "context": "For each row, the source derives the strict real bound m_i<x_i, where x_i=H*127^2*K^2/[126^2*(K-H)*S_i]. It then labels a vector of ceilings as exact strict-integer values.",
    "locator": "round-59-response.md, lines 222–239"
  },
  "witness": {
    "explanation": "The largest integer strictly less than x is ceil(x)-1, not ceil(x).",
    "evidence": "For integer m, m<x is equivalent to m<=ceil(x)-1. If x=2 exactly, the maximum admissible integer is 1, not 2; if x=2.3, it is 2, not 3. Consequently the printed thirteen ceilings must each be reduced by one to be sharp strict-integer maxima. Their use as conservative, larger deletion budgets may still be safe; the error is the exactness claim.",
    "scope": "computation_false"
  },
